 ## Overview
-3D Gaussian Splatting Training is the iterative optimization process that adjusts the parameters of a set of 3D Gaussian primitives to minimize the photometric difference between rendered images and ground truth views [S2][S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of reconstructing high-fidelity 3D scenes from 2D images by optimizing position, rotation, scale, opacity, and appearance (e.g., spherical harmonics) [S5, unofficial]. Unlike NeRF-based methods which optimize a continuous neural field, 3DGS training optimizes discrete geometric primitives, offering significantly faster training and inference speeds while achieving comparable image quality [S2][S5, unofficial]. It builds on [Training Optimization and Initialization](13464-training-optimization-and-initialization.md) by applying adaptive density control and gradient-based updates to the Gaussian parameters.
+3D Gaussian Splatting Training is the iterative optimization process that adjusts the parameters of a set of 3D Gaussian primitives to minimize the photometric difference between rendered images and ground truth views [S2][S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of reconstructing high-fidelity 3D scenes from 2D images by replacing implicit neural fields with explicit, differentiable Gaussian kernels [S5, unofficial]. Unlike NeRF-based methods which require volumetric sampling, 3DGS training relies on analytic rasterization of projected 2D Gaussians, offering significantly faster training and real-time rendering speeds [S2][S5, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from static initialization methods by actively modifying the primitive set (splitting, cloning, pruning) during optimization to balance reconstruction fidelity against computational cost [S2].
 
 ## How it works
